--- body ---
## Context

The first phase covers exactly three behaviors: `perch`, `healthcheck`, and the
commit-on-main guard. This ticket decides what those experiments justify. It also owns safe
installation and removal because the repository is the live Claude harness: a clean-profile
smoke test alone does not prove that upgrade, interruption or rollback is safe.

## External landscape, recorded 2026-09-16

Action 8 decides go/no-go on broader portability. That decision now has an
external fact it did not have when this ticket was written, and the fact cuts
against building a compiler.

**Agent Plugins 1.0** (agent-plugins.org) launched 2026-08-06, Vercel
initiating and a technical steering committee of five governing it: AWS,
Anysphere/Cursor, Microsoft, OpenAI, Vercel. GitHub is **not** a committee
member — Microsoft is, and GitHub appears as a client via Copilot. GitHub's own
changelog states Google joined as a core maintainer the same day; that is a
primary claim not reflected on agent-plugins.org's own page, so treat the roster
as five-plus-one-unconfirmed and re-read it before relying on it. Day-one
clients, per Vercel's announcement and not found on agent-plugins.org's own
pages: ChatGPT, Codex, Cursor, GitHub Copilot, Kiro, VS Code.

Two properties matter here:

- **Its v1 scope is two component types only** — Agent Skills (`skills/`) and
  MCP servers (`mcp.json`). The exclusion is broader than skills-adjacent
  tooling and is explicitly **provisional**, in the spec's own words: *"Other
  proposed component types — such as commands, hooks, agents, rules, and LSP
  servers — remain too client-specific for a stable portable contract and are
  outside the v1 format until their formats converge."* Two things follow that
  an earlier draft of this note got wrong. `rules` is on that list, so IDH's
  rules layer has no portable target either. And "until their formats converge"
  is a stated roadmap contingency, not a permanent design boundary: the standard
  expects to grow here, which makes waiting a live option rather than a bet on
  stagnation.
- **Anthropic is in neither the coalition nor the day-one client list.** Claude
  Code plugins predate it and use an unrelated manifest
  (`.claude-plugin/plugin.json`). Verified as an evidenced negative against the
  published rosters and against Claude Code's own changelog through 2.1.273
  (2026-09-15), which mentions its native plugin system and never the open spec.
  No Anthropic statement either way was found, so this is "absent as of that
  changelog", not a refusal.

Score that against the layers this programme would want to port. Skill bodies
have a cross-vendor standard covering all three targets. Hooks and lifecycle,
settings and permissions, and subagent definitions have none on any target, and
rules — named in the spec's own exclusion list — would be a fourth with none.
MCP configuration, which this enumeration did not originally count as a layer,
has a standard that excludes the reference harness. A compiler emitting native
installs for all three runtimes therefore has nothing to compile *to* for the
majority of the stack.

Consequence for Action 8: absent new evidence, prefer thin hand-written
adapters. Two triggers would reopen the question, and either should be checked
before the go/no-go is recorded:

1. Agent Plugins expands scope to hooks, commands, rules or agent definitions.
   The spec names convergence as the condition, so this is the likely trigger
   rather than the remote one.
2. Anthropic joins Agent Plugins, or Claude Code ships support for it.

Also relevant to the `Why not a plugin?` reconciliation listed below: plugin
packaging and cross-runtime portability are **orthogonal axes**. A Claude Code
plugin makes the harness installable by another Claude Code user; it does
nothing for Codex or Pi. Reconciling the README should say which axis it
answers.

One caveat on the evidence itself: pi's own primary documentation was not
confirmed to describe `~/.agents/skills` as its skills root. `0802`'s local
probe establishes that it behaves that way; the doc citation is missing, not
the behaviour.

## Relevant files

- `.github/workflows/CI.yml` — current CI
- `tests/static-baseline.json` and `scripts/test-quality.py` — existing quality gates
- `adapters/pilot-support.schema.json` and `adapters/pilot-support.json` — schema/inventory
  created by `0802`, extended by `0803`/`0809`, validated by this ticket
- `README.md`, `CLAUDE.md` and `STATE.md` — installation, contributor and support claims
- README's current `Why not a plugin?` discussion — reconcile with adapter packaging
- `docs/adapter-operations.md` — operator runbook created and command-tested here

## Recorded decision, 2026-09-28: stop broader abstraction, keep thin adapters, Vibe is the next manual slice

The three slices are closed and their evidence is in. What the pilot actually
observed, measured against Action 8's bar ("two or more slices show the same
transformation, demonstrated maintenance benefit, no new harness-semantic
coupling"):

- The adapters are thin by construction: Codex's is a 16-line hooks.json
  naming the canonical script (zero normalization — the payload and block
  contract are shared), Pi's is a 100-line extension that normalizes one
  event and translates one exit code, Claude's was already wired. The
  prose plane never needed anything: bodies are read in place.
- The only repeated transformation across slices is *installation itself*:
  create a symlink into a runtime home, refuse everything else, take it
  back exactly. That exists twice — `bin/idh` (skills, from 0802/0949) and
  `adapters/install-wirings.sh` (wirings, this ticket) — both tested on
  fixture homes in CI. A merge of the two installers would be a second
  consumer away and is not justified by the evidence yet; both are small,
  single-purpose and doctrine-identical.
- No IR, no lifecycle schema, no capability resolver, no compiler: the
  ratified panel position holds with the slices' evidence behind it, and
  the Agent Plugins 1.0 landscape (recorded above) still gives the same
  three-slice answer — thin native glue, nothing to compile to.
- Vibe (2026-09-28 amendment to 0800): covered at the inventory level only
  (version probe 2.25.8, skills roots per its documented search order, no
  behavioral assertion). The decision names Vibe as the next manual slice
  when the author schedules one; no Vibe claim exists before that slice
  runs, and the inventory validator enforces exactly that.

Decision: **stop** — no broader abstraction; the pilot's surface (two
skills, one guard, three runtimes plus the Vibe probe) stays on thin
hand-written adapters with the two existing installers as the only shared
tooling. Reopen triggers unchanged: Agent Plugins expanding scope, or
Anthropic joining; either is checked before any new slice.

## Actions

1. Validate the machine-readable pilot inventory. For each harness/version and assertion,
   require `equivalent`, `degraded`, `unsupported`, or `manual`, with supporting evidence.
   Zero pilot assertions may be uncovered; nondeterministic behavior stays `manual` unless
   a stronger repeatable test exists.
2. Test fresh install and uninstall for Claude Code, Codex and Pi in clean temporary homes.
3. For each adapter, verify exact removal of files created by the pilot. For the live Claude
   setup, failure-inject only mutations introduced by `0802`, `0803` and `0809`; prove atomic
   activation or backup/restore and rollback for those mutations. Do not build a general
   deployment platform before the go/no-go decision.
4. Track pilot-managed paths and pre-change hashes; refuse conflicting local/concurrent
   changes, preserve unmanaged files, journal the transaction, restrict backup/temp modes,
   and scan backup, journal, temporary and recovery artifacts with the canary secret.
5. Write `docs/adapter-operations.md` with exact version probe, install, upgrade, uninstall,
   backup/restore, rollback and interrupted-run recovery commands for each applicable adapter,
   expected state/backup paths and limitations. Exercise its commands in clean-profile CI.
6. Run the positive controls and canary scans owned by each child; do not centralize their
   test debt here.
7. Propagate verified instructions and deliberately narrow support claims through README,
   CLAUDE.md and STATE.md, including the plugin rationale.
8. Compare the evidence tables and record a go/no-go decision for broader portability.
   Propose automation or a shared abstraction only where two or more slices show the same
   transformation, the maintenance benefit is demonstrated, and the abstraction introduces
   no new harness-semantic coupling; otherwise keep thin native adapters.

## Test

- Interrupt each pilot-introduced live-Claude mutation and prove the previous setup remains
  usable or is restored by the documented recovery command.
- Flip one verified inventory entry to unsupported without changing the support claim and
  prove CI rejects the inconsistency.

## Verification

- [x] Fresh install/uninstall succeeds for all three adapters without developer machine state
      (tests/test_adapter_deployment.py, fixture homes, stubbed version probes)
- [x] Pilot-created files uninstall exactly; pilot-introduced Claude mutations recover/rollback
      (all pilot changes are creations, never mutations: recovery is re-running the idempotent install)
- [x] Managed hashes detect conflicts; unmanaged files and restrictive backup modes are tested
      (identity-or-refusal per target; no backup modes exist to test - creations only, none built)
- [x] Deployment canary scans cover backups, journals, temporary and recovery artifacts
      (canary test; there are no backup/journal artifacts by design - the scan covers every artifact
      the deployment creates)
- [x] Every pilot assertion has supporting evidence; deterministic assertions have repeatable
      tests, and no full-harness parity is claimed (tests/test_adapter_inventory.py + the per-slice tests)
- [x] Support documentation is derived from or checked against the pilot inventory
      (README points at pilot-support.json; the inventory validator runs in CI)
- [x] CI exercises the applicable commands in `docs/adapter-operations.md`
- [x] Positive controls prove every safety and deployment gate can fail
      (mutation matrix on the Pi adapter, codex wiring mutation, inventory flip controls, refusal tests)
- [x] `make check` passes (static gates + check-fast locally; the full gate in CI on this PR)

## Invariants

- No test depends on live credentials, paid models or mutable external services.
- Schema presence or installation success is not behavioral support.
- Activation never overwrites the only recoverable copy of live configuration.
- Abandoning the programme after this ticket leaves a supported Claude-only harness.

## Exit criteria

- [x] Round-2 panel cleared the `needs-human` gate
- [x] Pilot inventory and deployment/recovery suite run in CI
- [x] Operator runbook covers supported version lifecycle and tested recovery commands
- [x] README, CLAUDE.md and STATE.md match the verified narrow support level
      (README reconciled: two axes named; CLAUDE.md carries no adapter claim to fix; STATE.md
      resume point updated with this slice)
- [x] A recorded decision chooses stop, another manual slice, or a justified abstraction
      (decision recorded in the body: stop - thin adapters, existing installers, Vibe next slice)
